'''Amy Leigh Acton''' ([[Birth name|née]] '''Stearns''', formerly '''Beech'''; born February 16, 1966) is an American physician and researcher who is the [[Democratic Party (United States)|Democratic]] nominee for [[governor of Ohio]] in the [[2026 Ohio gubernatorial election|2026 election]].<ref>{{cite web |first= Madison |last= MacArthur |title= Democratic candidate Amy Acton wins primary election |url= https://spectrumnews1.com/oh/cleveland/news/2026/04/29/democratic-candidate-amy-acton-wins-primary-election |website= spectrumnews1.com |access-date= 6 May 2026}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |last= Evans |first= Nick |date= May 6, 2026 |title= Democrat Amy Acton and Republican Vivek Ramaswamy advance in Ohio election for governor • Ohio Capital Journal |url= https://ohiocapitaljournal.com/2026/05/05/democrat-amy-acton-and-republican-vivek-ramaswamy-advance-in-ohio-election-for-governor/ |website= Ohio Capital Journal |language= en-US |access-date= May 6, 2026}}</ref> 

Acton was born and raised in [[Youngstown, Ohio]]. She earned a bachelor's degree from [[Youngstown State University]], a [[Doctor of Medicine]] degree from the [[Northeast Ohio Medical University]], and a [[Professional degrees of public health|Master of Public Health]] degree from the [[Ohio State University]]. Acton served as the director of the [[Ohio Department of Health]], in which capacity she led [[COVID-19 pandemic in Ohio|Ohio's COVID-19 pandemic response]] from 2019 to 2020.<ref>{{cite web |last= Zuckerman |first= Jake |date= March 22, 2020 |title= Health dept. issues stay-at-home order; Ohioans to shelter in place through at least April 6 |url= https://ohiocapitaljournal.com/2020/03/22/dewine-issues-stay-at-home-order-ohioans-to-shelter-in-place-through-at-least-april-6/ |website= Ohio Capital Journal |language= en-US |access-date= March 12, 2026}}</ref> 

Before and immediately after serving as director of the Ohio Department of Health, she worked at the [[Columbus Foundation]]. She left the foundation upon announcing her candidacy in the 2026 Ohio gubernatorial election.

==Early life and education==
Amy Leigh Stearns was born on February 16, 1966, in [[Youngstown, Ohio|Youngstown]] and grew up in the north side of the city.<ref>{{cite web |url= https://www.democratsworkforamerica.org/candidates/2026/OH/Acton.html |title= Amy Acton |publisher= Democrats Work for America |accessdate= February 13, 2026}}</ref><ref name="Gordon-2020">{{cite web |url= https://www.cincinnati.com/story/news/2020/03/13/amy-acton-ohios-calming-leader-coronavirus-crisis/5044282002/ |website= [[Cincinnati.com]] |title= Amy Acton is calming leader in coronavirus crisis |last= Gordon |first= Ken |publisher= The Columbus Dispatch |date= March 13, 2020 |access-date= March 22, 2023}}</ref><ref name="Ellis-2020">{{cite web |url= https://www.cnn.com/2020/04/09/us/amy-acton-ohio-medical-director/index.html |title= Dr. Amy Acton, the Ohio Department of Health director, overcame a childhood in poverty to lead the state's battle against coronavirus |last= Ellis |first= Ralph |date= April 10, 2020 |publisher= [[CNN]] |access-date= April 16, 2020}}</ref>

Acton has said that after her parents divorced and while she lived with her mother, Donna Arthur, she experienced neglect, abuse, and periods of homelessness, including spending one winter living in a tent on the north side of Youngstown.<ref name="Miller-2020">{{cite web |last= Miller |first= Jessica |date= March 20, 2020 |title= Who is Ohio Department of Health Director Dr. Amy Acton? |url= https://www.wkyc.com/article/news/health/coronavirus/who-is-ohio-public-health-director-dr-amy-acton/95-930c562c-30ea-4cc9-96d6-0cf3d384247f |publisher= WKYC |access-date= March 25, 2020}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |title= Amy Acton responds to attack ad |url= https://www.wosu.org/politics-government/2026-10-20/amy-acton-responds-to-viral-video-where-her-estranged-mother-accuses-her-of-lying |publisher= [[WOSU-FM]]| first1= George| last1= Shillcock| first2= Allie| last2= Vugrincic |access-date= 22 October 2026}}</ref><ref name="Vindicator Mother">{{cite news |title= Acton's estranged mother calls daughter 'liar' |url= https://www.vindy.com/news/local-news/2026/10/actons-estranged-mother-calls-daughter-liar/ |work= The Vindicator |access-date= 22 October 2026}}</ref> Her mother remarried Cledith Arthur, who had already been prosecuted for raping his daughter; according to Acton and her younger brother, Phil Stearns, Arthur sexually abused her.<ref>{{cite news |title= Amy Acton's Pedophile-Protecting Mother Calls Her A 'Liar' |url= https://www.huffingtonpost.co.uk/entry/amy-acton-mother-ad-ohio-governor_n_6ad78dc6e4b02def98682767 |work= [[Huffington Post]] |access-date= 22 October 2026}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |title= Amy Acton's brother slams ad featuring estranged mother |url= https://www.usatoday.com/story/news/politics/2026/10/20/amy-acton-brother-slams-ad-estranged-mother/92348069007/ |first= Jessie |last= Balmert |work= [[Columbus Dispatch]], [[USA Today]] |orig-date= 2026-10-20 |date= 2026-10-21| access-date= 2026-10-24}}</ref> Arthur was charged with raping her when she was 12, but charges against him stalled after he fled the state and moved to Pennsylvania, despite several warrants being issued for his arrest over the next three years;<ref>{{cite news |title= Amy Acton's stepfather fled a rape charge. Prosecution was far from guaranteed in the 1970s |url= https://www.cleveland.com/crime/2026/10/amy-actons-stepfather-fled-a-rape-charge-prosecution-was-far-from-guaranteed-in-the-1970s.html |work= [[The Plain Dealer]] |access-date= 22 October 2026}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |last= Daniels |first= Cheyanne M. |date= October 20, 2026 |title= Ohio gubernatorial candidate Amy Acton's campaign condemns ad featuring her estranged mother |url= https://www.politico.com/news/2026/10/20/amy-acton-campaign-ad |website= [[Politico]] |access-date= October 20, 2026}}</ref><ref name="abc">{{cite web |url= https://abcnews.com/Politics/wireStory/democrat-ohio-governors-race-pushes-back-unattributed-ad-137424257 |title= Democrat in Ohio governor's race pushes back against unattributed ad featuring her estranged mother |agency= [[Associated Press]] |date= October 20, 2026 |publisher= [[ABC News (United States)|ABC News]] |first= Julie Carr |last= Smyth |access-date= }}</ref><ref name="wp-ad">{{cite web |url= https://www.washingtonpost.com/politics/2026/10/20/ohio-governors-race-uproar-over-video-featuring-democrats-estranged-mother/ |date= October 20, 2026 |first= Karen |last= Tumulty |title= Ohio governor's race in uproar over video featuring Democrat's estranged mother |website= [[The Washington Post]] |access-date= }}</ref> Acton's mother was later convicted of felony theft by deception.<ref name="Vindicator Mother" />

After Acton's mother lost custody of her,<ref>{{cite news |title= Donna Arthur, Ohio Democrat Amy Acton's estranged mom |url= https://www.al.com/politics/2026/10/democrats-estranged-mom-calls-her-liar-in-leaked-gop-attack-ad-experts-say-will-backfire.html |work= al.com |access-date= 22 October 2026}}</ref> Acton moved in with her biological father, Gerald Stearns, and found more stability. At [[Liberty High School (Ohio)|Liberty High School]], she was inducted into the [[National Honor Society]] and was named [[homecoming queen]] during her senior year in 1984.<ref name="Miller-2020" />

Acton paid her way through college, earning a bachelor's degree at [[Youngstown State University]]<ref>{{cite web |url= https://www.nbc4i.com/community/health/coronavirus/dr-acton-says-shes-an-ordinary-person-in-an-extraordinary-moment/ |title= Dr. Acton says she's an 'ordinary person in an extraordinary moment' |date= April 9, 2020 |publisher= NBC4 WCMH-TV |language= en-US |last= Boney |first= Stan |access-date= March 22, 2023}}</ref> followed by a medical degree from [[Northeast Ohio Medical University]] in 1990.<ref name="ThePulse-2019">{{cite web |url= http://thepulse.neomed.edu/news/amy-stearns-acton-to-lead-ohio-dept-of-health/ |date= March 4, 2019 |title= Amy Stearns Acton to lead Ohio Dept. of Health |website= The Pulse| publisher= Northeast Ohio Medical University |access-date= March 22, 2023}}</ref> After completing [[medical residency|residencies]] in [[pediatrics]] and [[preventive medicine]],<ref name="Schroeder-2020">{{cite web |url= https://www.daytondailynews.com/news/state--regional-govt--politics/coronavirus-who-amy-acton-leader-ohio-pandemic-response/adltAIcQBvznqpwNhewuwO/ |title= Coronavirus: Who is Dr. Amy Acton, leader of Ohio's pandemic response? |last1= Schroeder |first1= Kaitlin R. |last2= Bischoff |first2= Laura A. |date= March 16, 2020 |website= [[Dayton Daily News]] |archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20200317161049/https://www.daytondailynews.com/news/state--regional-govt--politics/coronavirus-who-amy-acton-leader-ohio-pandemic-response/adltAIcQBvznqpwNhewuwO/ |archive-date= March 17, 2020 |url-status= dead |access-date= }}</ref> she earned a master's in [[public health]] from [[Ohio State University]].<ref name="Gordon-2020" /> She then trained at [[Albert Einstein College of Medicine]]<ref name="ThePulse-2019" /> and [[Nationwide Children's Hospital]].<ref name="Gordon-2020" />

==Career==
Acton taught at [[Ohio State University]] as an associate [[professor of practice]] in public health, as well as at the Center for Injury Research and Policy at the Research Institute at Nationwide Children's Hospital in [[Columbus, Ohio|Columbus]].<ref>{{cite web |last= Beard |first= Kirsten |date= 2025-02-25 |title= CJN goes one-on-one with: Amy Acton as she seeks to become next governor |url= https://www.clevelandjewishnews.com/news/politics/cjn-goes-one-on-one-with-amy-acton-as-she-seeks-to-become-next-governor/article_3545e94a-f38a-11ef-ac3d-93b63940ba48.html |website= [[Cleveland Jewish News]] |access-date= 2026-10-24}}</ref><ref name="Gordon-2020" /><ref name="practice">{{cite web |last= Jones |first= Todd |title= A trusted pathfinder |url= https://alumnimagazine.osu.edu/story/trusted-pathfinder |website= Ohio State Alumni Magazine |date= October 28, 2025 |access-date= October 28, 2025}}</ref> She worked at the [[Columbus Foundation]]{{efn|The Columbus Foundation, founded in 1943, focuses on improving the quality of life in central Ohio by supporting community development, arts and culture, education health, and human services<ref>{{cite web |url= https://www.insidephilanthropy.com/find-a-grant-places/ohio-grants/columbus-foundation |title= Columbus Foundation |website= insidephilanthropy.com| access-date= }}</ref>}} as a grants manager<ref name="Bischoff-2019">{{cite web |url= https://www.daytondailynews.com/news/once-homeless-doctor-now-lead-ohio-health-department/HInM0S0k8RCkmP9s14iHrM/ |title= Once homeless, doctor now to lead Ohio Health Department |last= Bischoff |first= Laura A. |date= February 26, 2019 |website= Dayton Daily News |access-date= March 22, 2023}}</ref> and was director of Project LOVE (Love Our kids, Vaccinate Early), a childhood vaccination initiative started by local Columbus hospitals.<ref>{{cite web |last= Kaufman |first= Jane |date= 2022-05-05 |title= Acton to lead new Columbus-based nonprofit RAPID 5 |url= https://www.columbusjewishnews.com/news/state/acton-to-lead-new-columbus-based-nonprofit-rapid-5/article_0480656b-f505-5289-a972-9d557564083f.html |website= Columbus Jewish News |access-date= 2026-10-22}}</ref><ref name="Schroeder-2020" />

Acton was introduced to political organizing when she volunteered for the [[Barack Obama 2008 presidential campaign]]. She created a local email group for [[Bexley, Ohio]], called "Bexley, Yes We Can!" and publicized campaign events using Facebook.<ref name="stirland">{{cite magazine |last= Stirland |first= Sarah Lai |date= March 3, 2008 |title= Inside Obama's Surging Net-Roots Campaign |magazine= Wired |url= https://www.wired.com/2008/03/inside-obamas-surging-net-roots-campaign/ |issn= 1059-1028 |access-date= April 30, 2020}}</ref>

===Ohio Department of Health director (2019–2020)===
{{See also|COVID-19 pandemic in Ohio}}
[[File:Amy Acton.jpg|thumb|Acton with military dentist and U.S. Air National Guard Lt. Col. Timothy Stuhlmiller in 2019]]

In February 2019, Ohio governor [[Mike DeWine]], a [[Republican Party (United States)|Republican]], made Acton his final cabinet pick as director of the Department of Health.<ref name="Gordon-2020" /><ref name= "bbc41">{{cite news |date= April 1, 2020 |title= The US governor who saw it coming early |url= https://www.bbc.com/news/world-us-canada-52113186 |publisher= [[BBC News]] |language= en-GB |access-date= April 12, 2020}}</ref> Acton was the first woman in the post.<ref name="ThePulse-2019" /><ref>{{cite web |last= Kasler |first= Karen |date= February 27, 2019 |title= First Woman Appointed To Lead Ohio Department Of Health |url= https://radio.wosu.org/post/first-woman-appointed-lead-ohio-department-health |publisher= Ohio Public Radio |access-date= March 16, 2020}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |date= February 27, 2019 |title= DeWine Appoints First Female ODH Director |url= https://www.wcbe.org/post/dewine-appoints-first-female-odh-director |website= [[WCBE|wcbe]].org |access-date= March 22, 2023}}</ref> The two previous incumbents were a lawyer and a marketing director; DeWine mentioned wanting to "rethink how we approach this department".<ref>{{cite web |last1= Ludlow |first1= Randy |last2= Norwood |first2= Candice |date= February 28, 2019 |title= Once Homeless, Doctor Will Now Lead Ohio Health Department |url= https://www.governing.com/topics/health-human-services/tns-amy-acton-doctor-leads-ohio-doh.html |work= The Columbus Dispatch |via= www.governing.com |access-date= March 22, 2023}}</ref>

In 2020, before and during the [[COVID-19 pandemic in the United States|COVID-19 pandemic]], Acton advised DeWine, who became the first U.S. governor to close schools and limit gatherings to 100 people. Acton soon estimated that Ohio's then 5 confirmed cases likely translated to 100,000 actual cases, making national news.<ref name="Sullivan-2020">{{cite web |title= Ohio health official estimates 100,000 people in state have coronavirus |url= https://thehill.com/policy/healthcare/487329-ohio-health-official-estimates-100000-people-in-state-have-coronavirus |last= Sullivan |first= Peter |date= March 12, 2020 |website= [[The Hill (newspaper)|The Hill]] |access-date= March 22, 2023}}</ref> In mid-March, she predicted cases could peak in late April to mid-May.<ref>{{cite web |url= https://thehill.com/policy/healthcare/487684-illinois-ohio-closing-all-bars-restaurants-in-response-to-coronavirus |title= Illinois, Ohio closing all bars, restaurants in response to coronavirus |last= Conradis |first= Brandon |date= March 15, 2020 |website= [[The Hill (newspaper)|The Hill]] |access-date= March 16, 2020}}</ref>

On March 12, Acton said of the pandemic, "This will be the thing this generation remembers."<ref name="Sullivan-2020" /><ref>{{cite web |last= Bedo |first= Stephanie |title= 100k estimated to have virus in US state |url= https://www.dailytelegraph.com.au/lifestyle/health/ohio-health-official-estimates-100000-people-in-state-have-coronavirus/news-story/3535f6aa467279d9c2b81752ca4806b5 |website= The Daily Chronicle |date= March 13, 2020 |access-date= March 22, 2023}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |title= 'It doesn't seem real': What happens when coronavirus puts the world on lockdown |url= https://www.cincinnati.com/story/news/politics/2020/03/13/coronavirus-impact-how-life-changes-when-virus-forces-us-apart/5042637002/ |date= March 20, 2020 |orig-date= Originally published March 13, 2020 |last= Horn |first= Dan |website= [[The Cincinnati Enquirer]] |access-date= March 22, 2023}}</ref> [[Ohio House of Representatives|Ohio House]] Minority Leader [[Emilia Sykes]] called her "the real MVP of Ohio's coronavirus response".<ref name="Gordon-2020" /> The ''[[Dayton Daily News]]'' called her "Ohio's trusted face during the pandemic".<ref name="Schroeder-2020" />

Acton was an advocate of postponing the [[2020 Ohio Democratic presidential primary]], which was slated for March 17, 2020. The day before the scheduled election, DeWine canceled it, but a judge ruled he did not have the authority to do so. Acton then ordered polling places closed due to a public health emergency.<ref name="Polls Closed">{{cite news |url= https://www.whio.com/news/latest-election-still-tuesday-judge-says-governor-can-move/ApN0yeaA66RE3WPpeGIA2J/ |title= Coronavirus: Health director orders polls closed for primary election, citing emergency |date= March 17, 2020 |publisher= [[WHIO-TV]] |access-date= March 22, 2023}}</ref> It was later determined that the election would be conducted entirely by mail-in [[absentee ballot]] for those who had not participated in [[early voting]].<ref name="Absentee Ballot">{{cite news |url= https://wtov9.com/news/local/ohio-primary-election-absentee-ballot |title= Ohio residents will cast their vote in the primary election with an absentee ballot |first= Gage |last= Goulding |date= April 5, 2020 |publisher= [[WTOV-TV]] |access-date= April 7, 2020}}</ref> In April 2020, [[CNN]] called her "the Buckeye state's version of the straight-talking Dr. [[Anthony Fauci]]".<ref name="Ellis-2020" />

[[File:Double protest to Open Ohio 02aIMG 0789 (49872740076).jpg|thumb|Sign in support of Acton during pandemic]]

On April 1, DeWine was reported as "quick to defer to Dr. Acton for specific questions on the virus and its spread" during daily news briefings, "reminding Ohioans that the state's decisions are driven by science".<ref name="bbc41"/>

In May 2020, a group of 35 gyms sued the Ohio Department of Health, Acton, and the Lake County General Health District over COVID-related health restrictions, and Lake County Court of Common Pleas Judge Eugene Lucci issued a [[preliminary injunction]] blocking the state from "imposing or enforcing penalties solely for non-compliance with the director's order" against gyms and fitness centers, "so long as they operate in compliance with all applicable safety regulations".<ref name=Cass>{{cite web |first= Andrew |last= Cass |url= https://www.news-herald.com/news/ohio-officials-appealing-grant-of-preliminary-injunction-in-gym-lawsuit/article_4a6b0938-bf99-11ea-9a5d-df9add5394bd.html |title= Ohio officials appealing grant of preliminary injunction in gym lawsuit |publisher= The News-Herald |date= July 15, 2021 |orig-date= Originally published July 6, 2020 |access-date= March 22, 2023}}</ref> The state appealed the decision, but Acton had signed an order permitting gyms to reopen in the interim,<ref name="gymorder">{{cite web |title= Director's Order that Reopens Gyms, Dance Instruction Studios, and Other Personal Fitness Venues, with Exceptions |last= Acton |first= Amy |publisher= Ohio Department of Health |date= May 22, 2020 |url= https://coronavirus.ohio.gov/static/publicorders/order-to-open-gyms.pdf |access-date= June 30, 2022}}</ref> and a court of appeals later dismissed the case as moot.<ref name=Cass/><ref>{{cite court |litigants= Rock House Fitness, Inc. v. Himes |court= Ohio App. |opinion= 245 |date= 2021 |url= https://www.supremecourt.ohio.gov/rod/docs/pdf/11/2021/2021-Ohio-245.pdf |access-date= June 30, 2022}}</ref>

Beginning in May 2020, protesters began showing up at Acton's home in Columbus<ref name="protestors">{{cite web |date= May 3, 2020 |title= Small group of protesters gather in Dr. Amy Acton's neighborhood |url= https://www.news5cleveland.com/news/continuing-coverage/coronavirus/local-coronavirus-news/small-group-of-protesters-gather-in-dr-amy-actons-neighborhood |last= Justice |first= Camryn |publisher= [[WEWS]] |access-date= March 22, 2023}}</ref> and at press conferences. Acton was assigned a security detail.<ref>{{cite news |last1= Borchardt |first1= Jackie |last2= Balmert |first2= Jessie |title= First came the pandemic, then came the politics: Why Amy Acton quit |url= https://www.cincinnati.com/story/news/2020/06/12/why-ohio-health-department-director-amy-acton-quit/3174400001/ |publisher= The Enquirer |language= en-US |access-date= November 16, 2025}}</ref>

On May 20, 2020, the Ohio Senate unanimously voted against a proposal advanced by state House Republicans (and approved by the House on a nearly [[party-line vote]]) that would have limited the power of DeWine and Acton by restricting Ohio Department of Health orders to 14 days and requiring any extensions to be approved by a state joint legislative committee.<ref>{{cite web |first= Anna |last= Staver |url= https://www.cincinnati.com/story/news/2020/05/20/ohio-senate-unanimously-votes-down-house-proposal-slash-dr-amy-actons-authority/5231460002/ |title= Ohio Senate unanimously votes down House proposal to slash Dr. Amy Acton's authority |publisher= Columbus Dispatch |via= cincinnati.com |date= May 20, 2020 |access-date= March 22, 2023}}</ref> After Republican state legislators and then-Speaker of the House [[Larry Householder]] introduced bills that she believed would violate her [[Hippocratic Oath]],<ref name="Williams-2020">{{cite magazine |url= https://www.newyorker.com/news/us-journal/how-america-can-avoid-dual-cataclysms |last= Williams |first= Paige |date= November 1, 2020 |magazine= [[The New Yorker]] |title= How America Can Avoid Dual Cataclysms |language= en-us |access-date= March 22, 2023}}</ref><ref name="Parker-2025">{{cite web |last1= Parker |first1= Lisa |last2= Acton |first2= Amy |date= December 13, 2025 |title= She Didn't Quit. She Took a Stand. |url= https://substack.com/home/post/p-181296173 |via= Mamawsporchnpolitics |access-date= March 5, 2026}}</ref> Acton resigned on June 11, and became a chief health advisor for DeWine's administration.<ref name= Cass/><ref name="Deliso-2020">{{cite web |title= Amy Acton, Ohio's embattled health director, resigns amid COVID-19 crisis |url= https://abcnews.go.com/US/amy-acton-ohios-embattled-health-director-resigns-amid/story?id=71202353 |work= [[ABC News (United States)|ABC News]] |last= Deliso |first= Meredith |date= June 11, 2020 |access-date= March 22, 2023}}</ref> She has said that her resignation had nothing to do with protesters or the controversy over her and DeWine's stay-at-home orders.<ref name="Parker-2025" /> Lance Himes succeeded her as interim director.<ref name="Deliso-2020" /> In August, she announced she had left her position as advisor to the administration.<ref>{{cite web |last1= Evans |first1= Nick |first2= Paige |last2= Pfleger |title= Coronavirus In Ohio: Amy Acton Resigns As Governor's Chief Health Advisor |url= https://radio.wosu.org/post/coronavirus-ohio-amy-acton-resigns-governors-chief-health-advisor |publisher= WOSU Radio |date= August 4, 2020 |url-status= unfit |archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20200809102327/https://radio.wosu.org/post/coronavirus-ohio-amy-acton-resigns-governors-chief-health-advisor#stream/0 |archive-date= August 9, 2020 |access-date= March 22, 2023}}</ref>

Due to her work as the Director of the Ohio Department of Health, she received a [[Profile in Courage Award]] from [[Caroline Kennedy]] and [[Jack Schlossberg]]. ''The New York Times'' called her "The Leader We Wish We All Had".<ref>{{cite web |last1= Dosani |first1= Sanya |last2= Westbrook |first2= Adam |date= May 5, 2020 |title= The Leader We Wish We All Had |work= The New York Times |url= https://www.nytimes.com/2020/05/05/opinion/coronavirus-ohio-amy-acton.html |url-access= subscription |access-date= }}</ref>

===Nonprofit sector work===
After her resignation, she resumed working for the Columbus Foundation.<ref name="Williams-2020"/><ref>{{cite web |date= November 4, 2020 |title= Dr. Amy Acton breaks her silence about stepping down as Ohio's health director |url= https://www.nbc4i.com/news/local-news/dr-amy-acton-breaks-her-silence-about-stepping-down-as-ohios-health-director/ |publisher= NBC4 WCMH-TV |language= en-US |access-date= November 5, 2020}}</ref> On February 4, 2021, Acton stepped down from the Columbus Foundation as she considered a [[2022 United States Senate election in Ohio|run for the Senate in 2022]] to succeed [[Rob Portman]].<ref>{{cite web |url= https://www.cincinnati.com/story/news/politics/2021/02/04/amy-acton-steps-down-columbus-foundation-senate-run/4387971001/ |last= Borchardt |first= Jackie |website= Cincinnati Enquirer |title= Amy Acton steps down from Columbus Foundation to consider U.S. Senate run |date= February 4, 2021 |access-date= March 22, 2023}}</ref> In April 2021, she chose not to run and thanked Ohioans for an "outpouring of support".<ref>{{cite web |last= Koehn |first= Amanda |title= Acton opts not to enter Senate race |url= https://www.clevelandjewishnews.com/news/local_news/acton-opts-not-to-enter-senate-race/article_c3e034c6-9709-11eb-a08b-874482c9ae00.html |website= Cleveland Jewish News |publisher= Cleveland Jewish Publishing Company |date= April 7, 2021 |orig-date= Originally published April 6, 2021 |access-date= March 22, 2023}}</ref>

In 2022, Acton was named president and chief executive officer of RAPID 5, a nonprofit organization that attempts to improve access to parks in [[Franklin County, Ohio|Franklin County]], part of the greater Columbus area.<ref>{{cite web |title= 'Ultimate public health project:' Dr. Amy Acton looks to connect Franklin parks, waterways |last= Ferenchik |first= Mark |date= May 5, 2022 |website= The Columbus Dispatch |url= https://www.dispatch.com/story/news/2022/05/05/amy-acton-lead-new-effort-link-parks-trails-and-waterways/9626477002/ |access-date= June 28, 2022}}</ref> She left this position in May 2023.<ref>{{cite web |title= Amy Acton leaves as president and CEO of RAPID 5 parks and trails group in Franklin County |last= Ferenchik |first= Mark |date= May 9, 2023 |website= The Columbus Dispatch |url= https://www.dispatch.com/story/news/local/2023/05/09/amy-acton-leaves-as-president-and-ceo-of-rapid-5-group/70200956007/ |access-date= October 26, 2023}}</ref>

===2026 Ohio gubernatorial campaign===
{{main|2026 Ohio gubernatorial election}}
In the summer of 2024, Acton attended the [[2024 Democratic National Convention|Democratic National Convention]] in Chicago, where she first expressed interest in running for governor of Ohio.<ref name="The Statehouse News Bureau-2025">{{cite web |date= January 7, 2025 |title= Amy Acton, former health director, to run for Ohio governor as Democrat |url= https://www.statenews.org/government-politics/2025-01-07/amy-acton-former-odh-director-to-run-for-ohio-governor-as-a-democrat |publisher= The Statehouse News Bureau |access-date= January 19, 2025}}</ref> On January 7, 2025, she announced her candidacy in the [[2026 Ohio gubernatorial election]], filing paperwork earlier that day.<ref name="The Statehouse News Bureau-2025" /><ref name="AP News-2025">{{cite web |date= January 7, 2025 |title= Dr. Amy Acton, who helped lead Ohio's early pandemic response, joins 2026 governor's race |url= https://apnews.com/article/election-2026-governor-ohio-democrats-amy-acton-1c3c315b8534d3ac677fce3f77abca56 |publisher= Associated Press |access-date= January 19, 2025}}</ref> She is running as a Democrat.<ref name="AP News-2025" />

By the end of 2025, Acton had raised over $5.3 million for her campaign.<ref>{{cite web |title= Amy Acton fundraising surges as Democratic rivals exit |url= https://signalohio.org/amy-actons-fundraising-surges-democratic-field-clears-in-2026-ohio-governors-race-election-2026/ |website= Signal Ohio |date= January 30, 2026 |language= en-US |first= Andrew |last= Tobias |access-date= January 30, 2026}}</ref> In January 2026, Acton chose [[David Pepper (politician)|David Pepper]] as her running mate.<ref>{{cite news |url= https://thehill.com/homenews/campaign/5676722-ramaswamy-acton-running-mates-ohio-governor-race/ |title= Ramaswamy, Acton pick running mates in Ohio governor race |last= Mueller |first= Julia |date= January 7, 2026 |pages= 1 |work= The Hill |access-date= January 29, 2026}}</ref> In February, she addressed the affordability of everyday life, specifically healthcare, saying it is "the rising cost of health care that is really putting people over the edge" and citing a reported 20% decline in Marketplace enrollment in Ohio.<ref name="Evans-2026" /> She suggested tools such as [[Medicaid]] and tax credits for caregivers, but also emphasized the need for cooperation with the federal government to solve the issue.<ref name="Evans-2026">{{cite web |last= Evans |first= Nick |date= February 12, 2026 |title= Ohio Democratic governor candidate Amy Acton makes pitch for addressing health care costs |url= https://ohiocapitaljournal.com/2026/02/12/ohio-democratic-governor-candidate-amy-acton-makes-pitch-for-addressing-health-care-costs/ |website= Ohio Capital Journal |language= en-US |access-date= February 13, 2026}}</ref>

Acton was unopposed in the Democratic primary and faces Republican nominee [[Vivek Ramaswamy]] in the general election.<ref>{{cite web |title= Ohio Governor Primary Election 2026 Live Results |publisher= [[NBC News]] |date= May 5, 2026 |url= https://www.nbcnews.com/politics/2026-primary-elections/ohio-governor-results |access-date= June 4, 2026}}</ref>

In June 2026, after [[West Virginia v. B. P. J.|a U.S. Supreme Court ruling]] that upheld state laws barring [[transgender]] girls from girls' sports, Acton told ''[[The Blade (Toledo, Ohio)|The Toledo Blade]]'' that she did "not support boys playing in girls' sports", calling the issue settled law in Ohio and saying she would rather focus on affordability.<ref>{{cite web |date= July 13, 2026 |title= Some progressive Ohioans 'frustrated' by Amy Acton's comment on trans athletes |url= https://www.nbc4i.com/news/your-local-election-hq/some-progressive-ohioans-frustrated-by-amy-actons-comment-on-trans-athletes/ |publisher= [[WCMH-TV]] |language= en-US |access-date= July 15, 2026}}</ref> The statement drew criticism from the Ohio Democratic Progressive Caucus and the nonprofit Equality Ohio.<ref>{{cite web |last= Tobias |first= Andrew |date= July 15, 2026 |title= Top Ohio Democrats retreat on transgender issues, leaving some transgender activists feeling abandoned |url= https://signalohio.org/top-ohio-democrats-retreat-on-transgender-issues-leaving-some-transgender-activists-feeling-abandoned/ |website= Signal Ohio |language= en-US |access-date= July 31, 2026}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |last= Schneck |first= Ken |date= July 28, 2026 |title= Equality Ohio changes grade for Dr. Amy Acton from 'A' to 'Pending' |url= https://thebuckeyeflame.com/2026/07/28/equality-ohio-changes-grade-for-dr-amy-acton-from-a-to-pending/ |website= The Buckeye Flame |language= en-US |access-date= July 31, 2026}}</ref> In July 2026, Acton added language to her campaign platform committing to equal protection for [[LGBTQ+]] Ohioans and anti-discrimination laws covering [[sexual orientation]].<ref>{{cite web |last= Comeriato |first= H. L. |date= July 16, 2026 |title= Amy Acton adds LGBTQ+ equality language to campaign platform following anti-transgender controversy |url= https://thebuckeyeflame.com/2026/07/16/amy-acton-adds-lgbtq-equality-language-to-campaign-platform/ |website= The Buckeye Flame |language= en-US |access-date= July 31, 2026}}</ref>

In August 2026, Acton announced her support for [[2026 Ohio Issue 3|Issue 3]], a ballot measure that would add Ohio's existing photo voter identification requirement to the state constitution, breaking with most elected Democrats in Ohio. Acton said, "As this doesn't change what is already law, as governor, I will support it", while pledging to ensure all eligible voters could obtain the required identification easily and at no cost. Her position aligned with that of Ramaswamy, who had endorsed the amendment in May.<ref>{{cite web |last= Tobias |first= Andrew |date= August 7, 2026 |title= Amy Acton backs Issue 3, the voter ID amendment, breaking with Ohio Democrats |url= https://signalohio.org/amy-acton-backs-issue-3-voter-id-amendment-november-election-2026/ |website= Signal Ohio |language= en-US |access-date= August 8, 2026}}</ref>

On September 6, 2026, during Acton's appearance at the [[Canfield Fairgrounds|Canfield Fair]], a person armed with two handguns and a pair of [[brass knuckles]] lunged at Acton and injured multiple people before being subdued and arrested by her security detail; Acton was unharmed. According to the chairman of the [[Mahoning County, Ohio|Mahoning County]] Democratic Party, who witnessed the incident, the assailant shoved two campaign volunteers to the ground.<ref>{{cite news |url= https://www.wkbn.com/news/local-news/canfield-news/one-in-custody-after-altercation-during-amy-actons-fair-appearance/ |title= One in custody after altercation during Amy Acton's fair appearance |publisher= [[WKBN-TV|WKBN]] |last= Phillips |first= Brianna |date= September 6, 2026 |access-date= September 6, 2026}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |url= https://www.wlwt.com/article/amy-acton-attacked-at-campaign-event-near-youngstown/73625988 |title= Amy Acton attacked at campaign event near Youngstown; multiple people reportedly injured |publisher= WLWT |last= Knue |first= Kurt |date= September 6, 2026 |access-date= September 6, 2026}}</ref>

Acton has said she would cooperate with federal immigration enforcement as governor. After the Ohio Patrolmen's Benevolent Association endorsed her in August 2026, she said "we always work with the federal law enforcement," and News 5 Cleveland reported she would not stop local police from assisting [[United States Immigration and Customs Enforcement|ICE]].<ref name="news5">{{cite web |last=Trau |first=Morgan |date=2026-08-26 |title=Party flip? Democrat Acton supports ICE collaboration, as GOP Ramaswamy concerned about Flock cameras |url=https://www.news5cleveland.com/news/politics/ohio-politics/party-flip-democrat-acton-supports-ice-collaboration-as-gop-ramaswamy-concerned-about-flock-cameras |access-date=2026-10-27 |website=News 5 Cleveland}}</ref> She has said law enforcement should focus on violent criminals who are in the country illegally.<ref>{{cite web |last=Kreemer |first=Avery |date=2026-08-03 |title=Candidates on local ballots in Nov. weigh in on end of TPS for Haiti |url=https://www.daytondailynews.com/local/candidates-on-local-ballots-in-nov-weigh-in-on-end-of-tps-for-haiti/article_7c604aec-8dd8-59c8-8a11-7f09130ef0c8.html |access-date=2026-10-27 |website=[[Dayton Daily News]]}}</ref>

